  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Through comparative case studies in Favela Santa Marta (Rio de Janeiro) and Vila Tronco (Porto Alegre), this research documents how residents use participatory practices alongside, outside, and in opposition to state mechanisms to address transport injustices. The study maps everyday neighborhood actions that defend rights to mobility, local livelihood, and residency against conventional institutional planning framework.",
      "why": "It expands mobility justice frameworks beyond official government-led consultation processes by showing how informal and insurgent grassroots actions in the global South constitute active urban participation."
